// A tracing garbage collector over storage the caller supplies.
//
// Reference counting cannot free a cycle. A language whose programs build them -- a closure holding
// the scope that holds it, a parent holding children that hold it back, a handler registered on the
// object it belongs to -- needs something that traces, and an interpreter running somebody else's
// program cannot be told where to put a `weak` in advance. That is what this is for.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package:
//
//     dependencies {
//       gc { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/gc", version = "0.2.4" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate names the package, `gc`; the module a program imports is `sh.sysl.gc`. The module
// carries the `sh.sysl.` prefix so that no package claims a top-level word another one might want,
// and the package name is the short one because it names this repository rather than a namespace.

package {
  name    = "gc"
  version = "0.2.4"
}

// **`requires {}` is exact, and it is the whole reason to write this rather than bind one.**
//
// A capability clause says what a package asks of sysl, and this one asks for nothing: no allocator,
// no operating system, no clock, no files, no threads. It is handed a block of bytes and a function
// that greys the client's roots, and everything it does happens inside that block.
//
// **The hard part of a portable collector is root finding, not marking.** A conservative collector --
// Boehm's is the one everybody reaches for -- scans the machine stack, the registers and the static
// data segment for anything that looks like a pointer, and each of those is a per-target question
// answered by a linker script and a register spill. That is why such a collector is a porting
// project rather than a dependency, and why it is not something a sysl program can simply depend on
// and run on a phone, a Pico and a laptop.
//
// This one never scans anything. The client supplies its roots, because an interpreter already knows
// where they are: its value stack, its scope chain, its globals. What is left needs nothing from the
// machine, so the clause above is a fact rather than an aspiration -- and unlike a package that
// vendors C, there is no header to be missing on a freestanding target, because there is no C here
// at all.
//
// **What a hosted program does with that** is `malloc` one block at startup and hand it over. What a
// freestanding one does is point at a static array. Neither is a special case in this package.
requires {
}
